// ============================================================================
// Hunt: Claude Code "Don't Ask Again" approval leaked secrets into public
//   GitHub repositories via .claude/settings.local.json
// MITRE ATT&CK: T1552.001 (Unsecured Credentials: Credentials In Files)
// Data: Microsoft Defender DeviceFileEvents, DeviceProcessEvents,
//   DeviceNetworkEvents (Defender for Endpoint)
// Write-up: https://amitvijayan.com/journal/articles/daily-cyber-threat-brief-november-9-2026.html
// Logic: Apiiro's research (published November 5, 2026, by Shmulik Cohen
//   and Matan Giladi) found that approving a shell command in Claude Code
//   with "Yes, and don't ask again" stores the exact command text -
//   secrets included - as an allow rule in .claude/settings.local.json
//   inside the project folder. If that file is committed, the secret goes
//   with it. Apiiro scanned 104,185 public copies of the file on GitHub
//   (data collected November 1-3) and classified 1,122 of them (about
//   1.1%) as containing real secrets: 1,379 distinct values, including 379
//   database passwords, 23 AWS keys, 43 SSH logins, and 42 GitHub, GitLab
//   and npm tokens. Counting git history, shared settings files and agent
//   transcripts, the total rises to 2,539 secrets across 1,994
//   repositories and 63 organizations; 734 secrets sat only in git
//   history and 211 forks carried copies. One notified company confirmed
//   its exposed key was live. Standard scanners miss many of them:
//   Gitleaks flagged 564 of the 1,122 files and TruffleHog 643, while
//   neither flagged 215. Claude Code adds the file to the user's global
//   git excludes when it creates it, but that does not protect copies
//   already committed, clones, or other machines. Apiiro compared 16
//   coding agents and found Claude Code the only one confirmed to write
//   the full approved command into a project file by default. Apiiro did
//   not test the credentials, so this is exposure risk, not demonstrated
//   compromise; Anthropic had made no public statement as of November 8.
//   Variant 1 finds the settings file on endpoints - the prerequisite for
//   exposure. Variant 2 hunts git commands that stage, commit or push it.
//   Variant 3 takes devices with the file and looks for git pushes and
//   git.exe traffic to GitHub - the public-exposure chain. The queries use
//   no invented IoCs - the filenames and figures are the ones reported by
//   Apiiro. Remediation: search your GitHub organization for
//   .claude/settings.local.json and .claude/settings.json, rotate anything
//   found, and remember deleting the file does not help - history and
//   forks keep copies.
// ============================================================================

// -- Variant 1: Claude Code settings files present on endpoints --
// The allow rules live in .claude/settings.local.json (per-project) or
// .claude/settings.json (shared). Any machine with one of these files is
// a candidate for exposure - the file only becomes a leak if it is
// committed, but you cannot assess that without knowing where it exists.
DeviceFileEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(60d)
| where FileName in~ ("settings.local.json", "settings.json")
| where FolderPath contains ".claude"
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, ActionType, FileName, FolderPath, InitiatingProcessFileName, SHA256
| order by TimeGenerated desc

// -- Variant 2: git staging, committing or pushing the settings file --
// The exposure happens the moment the file is added or committed to a
// repository. Hunt git commands whose command line references the
// settings filename together with add, commit or push.
DeviceProcessEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(60d)
| where FileName =~ "git.exe"
| where ProcessCommandLine contains "settings.local.json" or ProcessCommandLine contains "settings.json"
| where ProcessCommandLine contains "add" or ProcessCommandLine contains "commit" or ProcessCommandLine contains "push"
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, FileName, ProcessCommandLine, InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine
| order by TimeGenerated desc

// -- Variant 3: git pushes to GitHub from devices carrying the file --
// Take every device with a Claude Code settings file and look for git
// pushes and git.exe network traffic to github.com - the chain that
// turns a local allow rule into a public secret. Pair any hit with
// Variant 2 to confirm the settings file itself went out.
let DevicesWithSettingsFile =
    DeviceFileEvents
    | where TimeGenerated > ago(60d)
    | where FileName in~ ("settings.local.json", "settings.json")
    | where FolderPath contains ".claude"
    | distinct DeviceName;
DeviceProcessEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(60d)
| where DeviceName in (DevicesWithSettingsFile)
| where FileName =~ "git.exe"
| where ProcessCommandLine contains "push"
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, FileName, ProcessCommandLine, InitiatingProcessFileName
| order by TimeGenerated desc
// -- git.exe network traffic to github.com on the same devices --
DeviceNetworkEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(60d)
| where DeviceName in (DevicesWithSettingsFile)
| where InitiatingProcessFileName =~ "git.exe"
| where RemoteUrl contains "github.com"
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, InitiatingProcessFileName, RemoteUrl, RemoteIP, RemotePort
| order by TimeGenerated desc
